/* ============================================================================
   COMMON MISTAKES - a companion to MEGA_STRESS_TEST.gcb

   This file does compile - every example below is a mistake someone
   new to GCBASIC might plausibly make, written as a comment showing the
   WRONG code, what actually happens when you compile it,
   and what to write instead. It exists to teach, and to record
   compiler behaviour that was deliberately tested - some of the compiler
   responses below are genuine gaps, noted as such.
   ============================================================================ */

#chip 18F46Q35
#option explicit

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   1) Forgetting "End If" / "Next" / "Loop" / "End Sub" / "Then"
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   All five of these are caught with a clear, correctly-located error:
     If x = 5 Then           -> Error: If without matching End If
       x = 6
     (no End If)

     For i = 1 To 10          -> Error: For without Next
     (no Next)

     Do                       -> Error: Do without matching Loop
       x = 1
     (no Loop)

     Sub Foo                  -> Error: Missing 'End Sub' or 'Return'
       x = 1                    definition or invalid 'GoSub'
     (no End Sub)

     If x = 5                 -> Error: If without Then
       x = 6
     End If
   These all handled well - GOOD. */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   2) "ElseIf" written as one word
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     If x > 100 Then
       ...
     ElseIf x > 10 Then        -> Error: Invalid syntax: Use ELSE IF
       ...
   GCBASIC requires the two separate words "Else If". Handled well - GOOD. */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   3) A mismatched/unclosed parenthesis
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     x = (5 + 3                -> Error: Missing parentheses error
   Catches the mistake (GOOD) but the message text itself has a wording
   bug - it reads like "...error" got appended twice. Cosmetic, not fixed
   here; a one-line messages.dat text fix whenever convenient. */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   4) Calling a Sub with too FEW arguments
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     Sub Foo(In first As Byte, In second As Byte)
       first = second
     End Sub
     Foo(1)                    -> Error: Missing value for parameter SECOND
   Handled well - GOOD - as long as the parameter names are more than one
   letter long (see #5). */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   5) Single-letter Sub/Function parameter names
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     Sub Foo(In a As Byte, In b As Byte)   -> Warning: Single letter
       a = b                                  variable name: A
     End Sub                                  Error: Invalid variable name: B
                                               - this single letter is
                                                 reserved for assembler
                                                 status/destination-bit
                                                 operands (B, C, F, H, N,
                                                 S, T, V, W, Z) ...
   This turns out to be INTENTIONAL, not a bug: B/C/F/H/N/S/T/V/W/Z (and I
   on AVR) collide with real assembler bit-instruction operand names, so
   the compiler has always rejected them. */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   6) A reserved keyword used as a variable name
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     Dim For As Byte            -> (build 1631 and earlier: compiled with
                                     NO error or warning at all!)
                                    (build 1632: Error: Reserved word
                                     cannot be used as a variable,
                                     parameter, or Sub/Function name: FOR)
   FIXED in build 1632. The first attempt at this fix checked every
   variable name against the compiler's full list of ~1000 reserved words
   - which also includes every library command name - and broke almost
   every program that uses a built-in function, because functions like
   ReadAD legitimately create a variable named after themselves to hold
   their own return value (Function ReadAD(...) uses a variable literally
   named READAD). That attempt was reverted. The real fix uses a small,
   separate, hand-picked list of about 38 true syntax keywords (For, Dim,
   Sub, Next, ...) that can never collide with a real function name. */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   7) Redeclaring the same variable with a different type
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     Dim x As Byte
     Dim x As Word              -> compiles with only unrelated "Single
                                    letter variable name: X" warnings -
                                    nothing about the redeclaration itself. */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   8) Writing past the end of a declared array
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     Dim arr(5) As Byte
     arr(99) = 1                -> compiles and assembles with ZERO
                                    warnings or errors. */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   9) Assigning a value that doesn't fit the variable's type
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     Dim MyByte As Byte
     MyByte = 300                -> Error: Cannot store 300 in the byte
                                     variable MYBYTE
     MyByte = -5                 -> Error: Cannot store -5 in the byte
                                     variable MYBYTE.  */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   10) Comparing a String variable directly to a bare number
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     Dim s As String
     s = "5"
     If s = 5 Then                -> Error: Pointer mismatch:  Cannot
                                      handle variable construct. Please
                                      check syntax, simplify source, check
                                      use of variable types, check
                                      String/Array size mismatch. Target
                                      variable = 5 from Main */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   11) Dividing by a literal zero
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     Dim MyByte As Byte
     MyByte = 5 / 0               -> compiles and assembles with ZERO
                                     warnings or errors.
                                     User should trap against this. */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   12) Forgetting the comma in a multi-value Case list
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     Select Case MyByte
       Case 1 2                  -> compiles with ZERO warnings or errors
         MyByte = 9                 - but the generated code checks for
       Case Else                    MyByte = 12 (!), not MyByte = 1 or 2.
         MyByte = 0
     End Select
     */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   13) Calling a Sub with too MANY arguments
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     Sub Foo(In first As Byte, In second As Byte)
       first = second
     End Sub
     Foo(1, 2, 3)                 -> compiles with ZERO warnings or
                                      errors; the extra argument (3) is
                                      silently dropped. */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   14) A Function that never sets its own return value
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     Function NeverSets() As Byte
       Dim Local As Byte
       Local = 5
     End Function                 -> compiles with ZERO warnings; the
                                      caller receives an undefined/garbage
                                      value.
                                    Alway set the retrun value */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   15) Overflowing a fixed-size String
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     Dim FixedString As String * 4
     FixedString = "ThisIsWayTooLong"   -> compiles with ZERO warnings.
   NOT FIXED. Since the literal's length is known at compile time (16
   characters into a 4-character buffer here), this could be checked for
   free too, at least for a literal string assignment specifically. */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   16) Writing to a port pin without setting its direction first
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     PORTC.0 = 1                  -> compiles fine, no error.
   This one is NOT considered a bug - a port has a real hardware default
   direction, and GCBASIC has never required Dir before every port write.
   Included here for completeness, not as a finding. */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   17) Using "While ... Wend" (a different BASIC dialect's loop syntax)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     While x < 5
       x = x + 1
     Wend                         -> Error: Do without matching Loop,
                                     then a cascading Syntax Error.
   GCBASIC has no While/Wend - use "Do While x < 5 ... Loop" instead (see
   the main tour file). The error does correctly reject the program
   (GOOD), though it describes the problem in terms of "Do" rather than
   naming While/Wend directly. */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   18) A missing "#include" for a chip family, chip, etc.
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   Now a compile ERROR (this was itself a bug found and fixed earlier in
   this session, r1925) - previously only a warning, silently compiling a
   program with that file's content missing. */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   19) A "0x"/"0b" numeric literal with no digits after the prefix
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     MyByte = MyByte Or 0x        -> Error: Invalid numeric literal 0x -
                                     no digits follow the prefix. */

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   20) Malformed "IntOn"/"IntOff" usage (e.g. with an argument)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
     IntOn Timer0Overflow         -> (build 1630 and earlier: HUNG THE
                                      COMPILER INDEFINITELY - no output,
                                      no error, forever)
                                     (build 1631+: Error: Syntax Error,
                                      in under 2 seconds. */
